            "text": "<p>What is the use for them?</p><p>If you do blind tastings, you know the struggle of what to wrap around your bottle. Some times bottles are wrapped in aluminium foil or a old tennis sock. It does simply not look good. This bottle cover is more professional looking, and does also support a good cause.</p><p>Production: </p><p>Materials are sourced locally in Denmark where <a href=\"https://champagne-season.com/blogs/all-blogs/bottle-covers-the-story-behind\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://champagne-season.com/blogs/all-blogs/bottle-covers-the-story-behind\">Lars</a> finds coffee roasters who are throwing away perfect coffee bags. And the raincoat material is also sourced from local a raincoat production. Then the raw materials are delivered to a socio-economic sewing workshop, where they cut and sew these bottle covers for your wine or champagne.</p><p>Bonus use:</p><p>It can also be used as a vase cover, so you can use a bottle as a vase, cover the bottle, and put a few flowers in the bottle.</p><p><a href=\"https://champagne-season.com/blogs/all-blogs/bottle-covers-the-story-behind\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://champagne-season.com/blogs/all-blogs/bottle-covers-the-story-behind\">Click here to learn more about the history.</a></p><p></p>",
